finish('l-versus-nl-directed-reachability',
 id='TCS-0004',title='Can directed reachability be decided in deterministic logarithmic space?',
 area='Computational and circuit complexity',criterion='models',question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''Is there a deterministic Turing machine A and an absolute constant C such that, for every directed graph G on vertices {1,...,n} and every s,t, A decides whether t is reachable from s while using at most $C\log_2(n+2)$ work-tape cells? G is supplied explicitly as a read-only adjacency matrix, together with s and t. The machine must halt and be correct on every input. This is equivalent to L=NL.''',
 definitions=r'''The graph may have self-loops; reachability allows a path of length zero, so s=t is a yes instance. Input access is two-way and read-only. Work tapes have a fixed finite alphabet; visited work-tape cells, including all counters and stored vertex identifiers, count toward space. The finite control and read-only input cells do not. There is no advice, random tape, oracle, writable copy of the input, or external storage. L is deterministic O(log N) work space on binary inputs of bit length N. NL uses the same space bound with nondeterministic transitions and accepts exactly when some computation branch accepts; all branches can be clocked to halt within polynomial time. Since the matrix encoding has N=Theta(n^2) bits, O(log N)=O(log n). A halting deterministic machine with this space bound automatically runs in polynomial time: it cannot repeat a complete configuration.''',
 answer_criterion='Give an always-correct deterministic logarithmic-space reachability algorithm for all directed graphs, or prove that no such algorithm exists. By NL-completeness under deterministic logspace reductions, either direction settles L versus NL. An algorithm for undirected graphs, a nondeterministic algorithm, or an algorithm using a larger power of log n does not answer the stated question.',
 context=[
  paragraph('A graph search usually remembers which vertices it has visited. With only logarithmic space, it can store a constant number of vertex names and counters, but cannot keep a separate visited bit for every vertex. The input may be rescanned as often as necessary. The challenge is to organize those repeated scans so that arbitrary directed paths can be detected without retaining a large search frontier.'),
  paragraph('Nondeterminism gives a simple logarithmic-space procedure: keep the current vertex and a length counter, guess the next edge, and accept if t is reached within n−1 edges. Any reachable target has a simple path within that bound. For a general nondeterministic logspace computation, vertices can instead represent machine configurations. An accepting computation becomes a directed path, explaining why this graph problem captures the whole class NL.', 'book'),
  paragraph('Savitch’s algorithm recursively asks whether a path can pass through a candidate midpoint. It saves space by recomputing subproblems, but a logarithmic recursion depth with logarithmic information per level uses O(log² n) space. Its running time is quasipolynomial. Reducing the storage to a single logarithmic factor would also force polynomial time in the stated machine model.', 'savitch'),
  paragraph('For undirected graphs, Reingold already achieved the logarithmic target. Edge direction is therefore a substantive part of this card. Likewise, NL=coNL shows that nonreachability has a nondeterministic logspace procedure; it does not remove nondeterminism. Recent connections with random-walk estimation make the landscape richer, while leaving the same deterministic directed question.', 'reingold')],
 why='Directed reachability is the canonical model of exploring possible computational states. Its space complexity determines whether nondeterministic logarithmic memory can be replaced by deterministic logarithmic memory, with consequences for low-memory graph algorithms and complexity theory.',
 importance=dict(score=94,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-10',reason='A central space-complexity separation expressed by a basic graph problem. The contrast with solved undirected reachability isolates a fundamental role of edge direction and nondeterminism.'),
 progress=[
  step('1970','Savitch proves that nondeterministic space s can be simulated deterministically in O(s²) space, for s at least logarithmic. Directed reachability consequently has an O(log² n)-space algorithm.','savitch'),
  step('1987–1988','Immerman and Szelepcsényi independently establish closure of nondeterministic space under complementation. In particular NL=coNL, so both reachability and nonreachability admit nondeterministic logspace algorithms.','complement'),
  step('2005–2008','Reingold obtains deterministic logarithmic space for undirected connectivity, published at STOC 2005 and subsequently in JACM. Arbitrary directed graphs are outside that theorem.','reingold'),
  step('2025','Doron, Pyne, Tell, and Williams give two algorithms such that, on each directed graph, at least one succeeds: deterministic polynomial-time, polylogarithmic-space transitive closure, or nondeterministic logarithmic-space random-walk estimation. This per-graph alternative does not give the first algorithm on every graph or reduce its space to O(log n).','winwin'),
  step('2026','Bhadra and Tewari show NL-hardness even for directed graphs whose edges can be partitioned into two forests of directed paths of length at most two, including when the partition is supplied. Such a simple decomposition therefore does not make the general logspace obstacle disappear.','sparse')],
 references=[
  ref('book','Mathematics and Computation','Avi Wigderson',2018,'https://www.math.ias.edu/files/mathandcomp.pdf','March 2018 draft, Section 14.1, Conjecture 14.8 and the configuration-graph interpretation'),
  ref('savitch','Relationships between nondeterministic and deterministic tape complexities','Walter J. Savitch',1970,'https://doi.org/10.1016/S0022-0000(70)80006-X','Journal of Computer and System Sciences 4(2), 177–192'),
  ref('complement','Nondeterministic Space is Closed under Complementation','Neil Immerman',1988,'https://doi.org/10.1137/0217058','SIAM Journal on Computing 17(5), 935–938; independently proved by Róbert Szelepcsényi'),
  ref('reingold','Undirected Connectivity in Log-Space','Omer Reingold',2008,'https://omereingold.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/sl.pdf','Author manuscript; JACM 55(4), DOI 10.1145/1391289.1391291'),
  ref('winwin','When Connectivity Is Hard, Random Walks Are Easy with Non-determinism','Dean Doron; Edward Pyne; Roei Tell; R. Ryan Williams',2025,'https://doi.org/10.1145/3717823.3718303','STOC 2025, Theorem 1 and introduction; open author version https://people.csail.mit.edu/rrw/NL_vs_BPL-3.pdf'),
  ref('sparse','Reachability in graphs having linear 2-arboricity two is NL-hard','Ronak Bhadra; Raghunath Tewari',2026,'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ipl.2025.106611','Information Processing Letters 192, article 106611; February 2026 issue, abstract')],
 status='source_open',status_note='The 2025 primary paper explicitly retains the directed logspace question, and the February 2026 hardness result concerns restricted graph classes without resolving it. Searches through 10 September 2026 did not locate a resolution of L versus NL.',
 formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
